<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    GH-890 (follow-up to GH-888). Stage P5, type propagation: option floatbits
    (kuna_floatbits.rs), default on.

    A float helper that only works on the bits of its float-register input is
    a float on both sides. newlib's fabsf is GET_FLOAT_WORD / SET_FLOAT_WORD:
    hard-float ARM compiles it to vmov r0,s0; bic r0,r0,#0x80000000; vmov s0,r0,
    and x86-64 gcc -O2 to movd %xmm0,%eax; and $0x7fffffff,%eax; movd %eax,%xmm0.
    Every op on the value is an integer op, so the fold typed the helper
    unsigned int (unsigned int), a prototype C passes in r0 / edi, and each
    caller printed the call as a reinterpretation of its result.

    The XML wrapper only provides the harness architecture. The script loads
    three in-tree fixtures built from floatbitshelper.c (source beside them): clang
    -O2 for Cortex-M4F hard-float, gcc -O2 for x86-64, clang -O0 for AArch64.
    Pass 1 (option floatbits off) reproduces the integer prototypes; pass 2
    (on, the default) types fb_fabsf, fb_negf and fb_copysignf float on both
    sides and fb_fabs double (x86-64, AArch64), and the classifier fb_isnanf, which answers in
    an integer register, takes a float parameter only. fb_bump adds 1 to the
    bits, fb_store stores them, and fb_mix ors in an integer parameter before
    returning them in s0 / xmm0: all three keep their integer types, and
    fb_mix keeps them on both sides.

    The last pass loads floatbitsrefuse.c built for the same three targets
    (clang -O2 on AArch64). An == or != of the bits prints as a float
    comparison once the input is a float, so it keeps the integer unless the
    other side is a normal number or an infinity: zero (-0.0 == 0.0), a NaN
    pattern and another input's bits refuse. A NaN payload or 0xffffffff
    merged into the returned value would print as NAN and refuses; the
    canonical NaN spells. An == with a float constant passes only when the
    literal the printer writes, which C reads as a double, is that float:
    1.0 and 16777218.0 do, 0.1f, FLT_MAX, FLT_MIN, 1.0000001f and
    0.33333334f do not. The low word of a _Float128 compared with zero or with
    its bit 63 flipped, the second float of a struct in xmm0 with its sign
    flipped, and two integer lanes masked with 0x7fffffff7fffffff keep their
    integers.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
